<DocPage eyebrow="GUIDE / WORLD OBJECT TREE" title="Publish the game world as semantic information" lead="World Object Tree turns non-UI game state—doors, enemies, objectives, items, and checkpoints—into named, typed objects that tests and AI agents can understand." description="World Object Tree concepts, Godot and Unity setup, selectors, MCP, Inspector, and visibility rules." section="world-object-tree">
  <h2>What is World Object Tree?</h2><p>Semantic UI Tree describes interface elements such as buttons, labels, and input fields. World Object Tree describes the game world outside that UI: for example, “the reactor door is locked,” “enemy A is active,” or “checkpoint B is available.” It gives each published object a stable identity and semantic state without exposing the engine's complete scene graph.</p>
  <Callout title="Why objects are selected explicitly and kept observation-only"><p>A scene contains far more than meaningful gameplay targets: decorative grass, particles, lights, helper nodes, pooled objects, and internal state. Publishing all of it would make every blade of grass look like an item, create noisy and unstable data, and risk exposing implementation details. Explicit opt-in keeps only concepts the game intentionally defines. The tree is read-only so an agent cannot mutate arbitrary scene objects; bounded operations belong in Semantic UI actions or an explicit Semantic Game Action map.</p></Callout>
  <h2>What an object contains</h2><p>World Object Tree v1 stores a stable <code>id</code>, semantic <code>kind</code>, optional label and parent, 2D or 3D position, active and player-visible state, tags, and flat primitive state. State values are strings, finite numbers, booleans, or null.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Godot: publish an opted-in Node2D" lang="gdscript" code={`$Door.add_to_group(&"gua_world_object")
$Door.set_meta(&"gua_world_id", "door-a")
$Door.set_meta(&"gua_world_kind", "door")
$Door.set_meta(&"gua_world_label", "Reactor door")
$Door.set_meta(&"gua_world_visible_to_player", true)
$Door.set_meta(&"gua_world_state", {"locked": true})`} />
  <h2>Godot and Unity</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Godot</strong><p>Add a <code>Node2D</code> or <code>Node3D</code> to the <code>gua_world_object</code> group and supply <code>gua_world_*</code> metadata. IDs must be unique and parent references must resolve inside the same published frame.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Unity</strong><p>Add <code>GuaWorldObject</code> to an active GameObject and configure its ID, kind, visibility, tags, and state. The adapter publishes only registered components.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Query and wait</h2><p>MCP exposes <code>get_world_object_tree</code>, <code>find_world_objects</code>, and <code>wait_for_world_object</code>. Selectors can match ID, kind, label, tags, state, ancestor, activity, and visibility. Supply <code>relativeToObjectId</code> and <code>maxDistance</code> to search around a reference object, with an optional positive <code>limit</code>. Inspector renders the tree in its separate World Object Tree panel.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Find nearby published enemies" lang="json" code={'{\n  "kind": "enemy",\n  "relativeToObjectId": "player",\n  "maxDistance": 12,\n  "limit": 5\n}'} />
  <p>Nearby search uses one projected World snapshot: XY Euclidean distance for <code>world2d</code> and XYZ for <code>world3d</code>. The maximum is inclusive, the reference object is excluded, and results are ordered by distance then object ID before the limit is applied. Distances use the host-defined world unit, so a scene must publish positions and radii in one consistent unit convention.</p>
  <p>Every successful query returns <code>sessionEpoch</code>, <code>frameSequence</code>, and <code>revision</code>. A nearby result also includes <code>spatial.distances</code> aligned with its returned matches and <code>truncated</code> when the limit omitted further matches.</p>
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Where world snapshots and waits live"><p>See <a href="/state-and-wait/">How Gua manages state, actions, and waits</a> for how the engine remains the source of truth while Gua Runtime publishes a current view and condition waits read fresh snapshots.</p></Callout>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Observation, not world mutation"><p>World v1 has no world-action tools. Operate UI semantically or publish a bounded Semantic Game Action instead of exposing arbitrary scene mutation.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Player-safe projection</h2><p>The bridge defaults to Debug. Set <code>GUA_OBSERVATION_PROFILE=player</code> in a player-facing host so UI and World data are projected before any transport receives them. Nearby search resolves its reference only after projection; private, unknown, and coordinate-omitted references therefore produce the same empty result and reveal no distance. A client cannot request an upgrade to Debug. Continue with the <a href="/agent-policy/">Agent exposure policy guide</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="physics">World radius search versus physics queries</h2><p><code>relativeToObjectId</code>/<code>maxDistance</code> searches the projected positions of published World objects. It does not raycast against colliders. Physics queries need a separately bound and explicitly authorized Testing/Debug provider. Local managed adapters use <code>Gua.Runtime.GuaRuntimeSpatialClient</code>; external .NET tests use <code>Gua.Testing.GuaWebSocketContext.GetSpatialInfo</code>/<code>QuerySpatialBatch</code>; <code>gui-mcp</code> uses <code>get_spatial_info</code>/<code>query_spatial_batch</code>.</p><p>The tested providers are Godot 4.7 GodotPhysics3D/Jolt and Unity 6000.5.3f1 desktop Mono/PhysX. Reset, scene/provider changes, and revoked access invalidate requests; keep query count and result bounds within the provider's advertised limits. Built-in browser WebMCP privileged queries are unsupported. <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/docs/spatial-engine-r1.md">Provider setup and limits</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="typescript">Use World types in TypeScript</h2><p>Use <code>gua-world-tools@latest</code> to validate object trees and build selectors. Use <code>gua-value-tools</code> to compare additional typed observations. World state uses ordinary strings, numbers, booleans, or null rather than typed Value JSON. See <a href="/docs/npm-packages/">npm installation and the door/inventory examples</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
